From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 20 Jul 2026 12:35:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] soc: cix: acpi-resource-lookup: validate RSTL entries

reset_controller_add_lookup() requires both dev_id and provider.  The CIX
RSTL parser used a different condition: it admitted a NULL dev_id whenever
con_id was present.  Reset core then rejected the unusable entry and emitted

  reset lookup entry badly specified, skipping

three times while parsing the Orion O6N firmware tables.

Make the parser enforce reset core's actual contract before registration.
This does not discard a usable con_id-only lookup: reset lookups first match
dev_id against dev_name(dev), then use con_id to select a reset for that
consumer, so dev_id is mandatory.

The ACPI-name fallback in acpi_obj_to_devname() (already present in this
v7.2/ncz tree, backported to v7.0.12/stable by the sibling patch 2026)
handles platform devices that have not yet been instantiated.  Therefore
a NULL name here means the O6N RSTL provider/consumer reference itself
did not resolve to an ACPI device, not a normal probe-ordering case.
Skip that invalid firmware entry at the parser boundary and retain a
targeted dev_dbg diagnostic instead of passing it to the generic reset
core warning.

Same fix class as the night's -ENOENT-to-EPROBE_DEFER clock fixes --
O6N-specific (the identically-built MS-R1 host on .66 shows zero of these
warnings, confirmed by `cat /proc/device-tree/model` = "MS-R1"), not a
generic kernel bug.  Likely O6N firmware/ACPI-table gaps relative to MS-R1's
more mature board support.

Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/soc/cix/acpi/cix-acpi-resource-lookup-v1.c b/drivers/soc/cix/acpi/cix-acpi-resource-lookup-v1.c
index a7525e3..91a58e2 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/cix/acpi/cix-acpi-resource-lookup-v1.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/cix/acpi/cix-acpi-resource-lookup-v1.c
@@ -126,8 +126,18 @@ static int reset_lookup_handle(acpi_handle handle,
 			devm_kstrdup(dev, el[3].string.pointer, GFP_KERNEL)
 			: NULL;
 
-		if (!lookup->provider || (!lookup->dev_id && !lookup->con_id))
+		/*
+		 * reset_controller_add_lookup() requires both fields, and lookup
+		 * matching starts with dev_id before considering con_id.  The ACPI
+		 * name fallback above covers consumers not instantiated yet, so a
+		 * missing name here denotes an unresolved firmware reference.
+		 */
+		if (!lookup->provider || !lookup->dev_id) {
+			dev_dbg(&adev->dev,
+				"invalid RSTL[%d]: provider[%s] dev[%s] con[%s]\n",
+				i, lookup->provider, lookup->dev_id, lookup->con_id);
 			continue;
+		}
 
 		reset_controller_add_lookup(lookup, 1);
 
-- 
